# LeakSanitizer suppressions.
#
# One entry, and it should stay that way: everything the project itself
# allocates is expected to be freed, and a suppression file that grows is a
# suppression file that stops being read.
#
# OpenSSL 3 keeps a public and a private DRBG per thread, created on first use -
# here from SSL_CTX_new, inside libdatachannel's DTLS transport - and frees them
# when the thread exits. A process that ends while a worker thread still holds
# its pair hands LeakSanitizer two live allocations with no owner left to point
# at, which is what a leak looks like from the outside. It reported 4848 bytes
# in 18 allocations: two roots in rand_new_drbg and sixteen children of them,
# every frame inside OpenSSL or libdatachannel and not one in this repository.
#
# It is also why SfuTest.EachParticipantGetsOneTrackPerOtherParticipant and
# SfuTest.VideoAndAudioTravelWithoutGettingMixedUp failed on one run and passed
# on the one before it with no change in between: whether the DTLS thread has
# exited by the time LeakSanitizer looks is a matter of timing.
#
# Matching rand_new_drbg rather than SSL_CTX_new keeps this to the DRBG
# allocation itself. A leak of ours reached through SSL_CTX_new still fails.
leak:rand_new_drbg
